---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import ButtonDemo from "../../components/ButtonDemo";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
---

<Docs
  title="Button"
  description="Compact buttons and links for actions, toggles, and triggers."
>
  <h1 class="docs-title">Button</h1>
  <p class="docs-lead">
    Compact actions, links, and toggles. Seven variants, sizes for common
    layouts, and Base UI focus and disabled behavior.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-8"><ButtonDemo client:load /></div>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Usage</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="Button usage"
      code={`import { Button } from "@tulipfarm/sprout/button";

<Button onClick={save}>Save</Button>
<Button variant="outline" size="sm">
  <PlusIcon data-icon="inline-start" />
  Add item
</Button>
<Button variant="ghost" size="icon-sm" aria-label="Delete file">
  <TrashIcon />
</Button>`}
    />
    <p>
      Variants are <code>default</code>, <code>outline</code>, <code
        >secondary</code
      >, <code>ghost</code>, <code>destructive</code>, <code>link</code>, and <code
        >scope</code
      >. Sizes are <code>default</code>, <code>xs</code>, <code>sm</code>, <code
        >lg</code
      >, and the square <code>icon</code>, <code>icon-xs</code>, <code
        >icon-sm</code
      >, and <code>icon-lg</code>. Give icon-only buttons an <code
        >aria-label</code
      >. Mark leading or trailing icons with <code
        >data-icon="inline-start"</code
      > or <code>"inline-end"</code> to tighten padding.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Links</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="Router link"
      code={`<Button
  variant="outline"
  nativeButton={false}
  render={<Link to="/files" />}
>
  Open files
</Button>`}
    />
    <p>
      With <code>nativeButton={false}</code>, Button renders a real link. It
      keeps link semantics, so screen readers announce a link and Enter follows
      it. A disabled link gets <code>aria-disabled</code>, leaves the tab order,
      and ignores clicks.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">States</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="Invalid and pressed"
      code={`<Button aria-invalid aria-describedby="owner-error">Assign</Button>

<Button
  variant={selected ? "secondary" : "ghost"}
  aria-pressed={selected}
  onClick={toggle}
>
  Board
</Button>`}
    />
    <p>
      Button styles <code>disabled</code>, <code>aria-disabled</code>, <code
        >aria-busy</code
      >, <code>aria-invalid</code>, <code>aria-expanded</code>, and <code
        >aria-pressed</code
      >. Your application owns the error text and the pressed state. For busy
      and blocked actions, pass <code>state</code> as shown below.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Action states</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="Ready, busy, and blocked"
      code={`import { Button, type ButtonState } from "@tulipfarm/sprout/button";

const state: ButtonState = sending
  ? { kind: "busy" }
  : !enabled
    ? {
        kind: "blocked",
        reason: "Turn on Enable execution first.",
        fix: { kind: "jump", label: "Go to Enable execution", target: "enable-execution" },
      }
    : { kind: "ready" };

<Button state={state} pendingLabel="Activating…" onClick={activate}>
  Activate saved instructions
</Button>`}
    />
    <p>
      Pass <code>state</code> when an action can be busy or blocked. Busy sets
      <code>aria-busy</code> and, with <code>pendingLabel</code>, swaps the
      label for a spinner and that text. Busy and blocked buttons are disabled
      but stay in the tab order, so keyboard users reach them and hear the
      reason through <code>aria-describedby</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      With <code>state</code>, Button renders a <code
        >data-slot="button-action"</code
      > wrapper with <code>data-state</code> that also holds the reason, and
      <code>className</code> styles that wrapper. Keep passing a state, even
      <code>{`{ kind: "ready" }`}</code>, for the button's lifetime. Changing
      its kind then keeps the same button element and its focus. Long labels
      wrap in <code>default</code>, <code>sm</code>, and <code>lg</code>
      sizes.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="Blocker fixes"
      code={`type ButtonBlockerFix =
  | { kind: "jump"; label: string; target: string }   // element id on this page
  | { kind: "link"; label: string; link: ReactElement } // your router <Link>
  | { kind: "action"; label: string; onClick: () => void };`}
    />
    <p>
      A <code>jump</code> fix calls <code>jumpTo</code>, which scrolls to the
      element and focuses it, or its first focusable child. A <code>link</code>
      fix renders your link through <code>render</code>. An <code>action</code>
      fix runs a callback, such as Try again. Leave <code>fix</code> out to show only
      the reason. <code>useActionControl(state)</code> returns the same button props
      and reason for controls you build yourself.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Action groups</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="ActionGroup"
      code={`import { ActionGroup } from "@tulipfarm/sprout/button";

<ActionGroup
  state={reviewState}
  actions={[
    { label: "Keep my draft", variant: "outline", onClick: keepDraft },
    { label: "Use current settings", variant: "outline", onClick: adoptCurrent },
  ]}
/>`}
    />
    <p>
      <code>ActionGroup</code> puts several actions under one state. Every button
      turns busy or blocked together, and one reason sits under the row.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Triggers and class-only styling</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="Base UI trigger"
      code={`import { Menu } from "@base-ui/react/menu";
import { Button, buttonVariants } from "@tulipfarm/sprout/button";

<Menu.Trigger render={<Button variant="outline" size="sm" />}>
  Sort
</Menu.Trigger>

<a className={buttonVariants({ variant: "ghost", size: "sm" })} href={url}>
  Open in calendar
</a>`}
    />
    <p>
      Pass Button to any Base UI trigger or close part through <code
        >render</code
      >. The trigger supplies its ARIA state and Button styles it. Use <code
        >buttonVariants()</code
      > when another component owns the element. Tailwind utilities passed through
      <code>className</code> override Button's own styles.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Pattern sizes</h2>
    <p>
      Pattern sizes fit common layouts: <code>back</code> (icon-only below 640px when
      its label is hidden), <code>onboarding</code>, <code>bud</code> and <code
        >bud-labeled</code
      > floating launchers, <code>chat-row</code>, <code>task-link</code>, <code
        >card-title</code
      >, <code>result-card</code>, <code>calendar-title</code>, <code
        >calendar</code
      >, <code>calendar-icon</code>, <code>nav</code> (tree rows), <code
        >column-header</code
      >, <code>column-header-end</code>, <code>table-link</code>, and <code
        >badge</code
      >. <code>buttonVariantNames</code> and <code>buttonSizeNames</code> list every
      option.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Behavior and accessibility</h2>
    <p>
      Buttons default to <code>type="button"</code>; pass <code
        >type="submit"</code
      > or <code>"reset"</code>, and <code>form</code> for an external submit. Tab
      reaches every enabled button. Enter and Space activate buttons; Enter follows
      links. Focus shows a 2px ring. Coarse pointers get 44px minimum targets. Hover
      and press transitions take 120ms and stop when the user prefers reduced motion.
    </p>
    <p>
      Button renders <code>data-slot="button"</code>, <code>data-variant</code>,
      and <code>data-size</code>. Override <code>data-slot</code> when a wrapping
      component needs its own hook. Refs reach the rendered element.
    </p>
  </section>
</Docs>
